[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: What goes in a context packet, and what happens when it does not fit

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READY-PACKET-BUDGET
TITLE: What goes in a context packet, and what happens when it does not fit
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
`ready.py --packet` renders a whole closure as one brief and nothing bounds it. Measured over the current export: the deepest closure is `SLICE-INSTANCE-SEMANTICS-MIGRATION` at 34 of 144 nodes carrying 207,174 characters of field text against 365,154 for the whole graph — 57 percent of the corpus in one packet. Restricting the packet to the contract fields — UID, TITLE, STATEMENT, DEPTH, STATUS, RETIRES_ON, AUTHORED_BY — cuts the same closure to 77,039 characters, a 63 percent reduction, because NOTES and RATIONALE carry most of the bytes. Decide whether NOTES and RATIONALE belong in a packet at all, given fp-check already excludes them from the contract hash, and decide what the packet does when a budget is exceeded: truncate at a hop distance, drop non-contract fields, or refuse.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The packet is what hands an implementing session its brief, so its content is spent on every session that uses it, and the lever is measurable rather than stylistic. `MECH-READY-QUERY` settles the order — dependency-first — and the record shape, and says nothing about content or size; at 144 nodes the whole-closure packet fits a session, and it is already more than half the graph.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 by summing every string field per node over a clean export, which is a different basis from the 59,740 characters `MECH-READY-QUERY`'s NOTES records for the same slice through `render.py`; the ratio between the two bases is the measurement that matters here, not either absolute.
<<<
